.TH MNT 9
.SH NAME
mnt \- the 9P transport: tag pool, workers, flush, payload buffers, the wire
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "kernel:mnt"
.PP
.nf
.B "init :: proc(c: ^Conn, handler: vectra9.Handler, server: rawptr, abort: proc, payload: []u8) -> bool"
.B "serve_start :: proc(c: ^Conn, n: int) -> bool"
.B "serve_stop :: proc(c: ^Conn)"
.B "transport :: proc(c: ^Conn) -> vectra9.Transport"
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B "call :: proc(c: ^Conn, request, reply: ^vectra9.Msg, buf: []u8) -> vectra9.Error"
.B "call_for :: proc(c: ^Conn, request, reply: ^vectra9.Msg, ticks: u64, buf: []u8) -> vectra9.Error"
.B "flushed :: proc(c: ^Conn, tag: vectra9.Tag) -> bool"
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B "wire_init :: proc(w: ^Wire, io: Wire_IO, arena: []u8) -> bool"
.B "wire_start :: proc(w: ^Wire) -> bool"
.B "wire_join :: proc(w: ^Wire)"
.B "wire_join_for :: proc(w: ^Wire, ticks: u64) -> bool"
.B "wire_call :: proc(w: ^Wire, request, reply: ^vectra9.Msg, buf: []u8) -> vectra9.Error"
.B "wire_call_for :: proc(w: ^Wire, request, reply: ^vectra9.Msg, ticks: u64, buf: []u8) -> vectra9.Error"
.B "wire_broken :: proc(w: ^Wire) -> bool"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Mnt
is the transport a
.IR vfs (9)
.B Server
moves onto when a synchronous call -- the handler running on the caller's
own stack -- is not enough: several requests in flight at once, a caller
that can give up on one, and a handler that answers from another thread.
.B Conn
is the in-kernel half, a work queue and a pool of worker threads over a
handler this package can call directly.
.B Wire
is the same mechanism over a byte stream, for a 9P server that is a ring 3
process at the other end of a pipe.
.SS Requests in flight
A connection holds at most
.B MAX_REQUESTS
(8) requests at once, and the tag pool
.I is
the request pool: slot
.I i
carries tag
.IR i .
A
.B Tflush
names a request by tag, so the pool has to be indexable by tag for a flush
to find anything.
The pool is actually
.B POOL
(twice
.BR MAX_REQUESTS ),
because the upper half is reserved: slot
.I i
.B "+ MAX_REQUESTS"
is the flush partner of slot
.IR i ,
and belongs to whoever owns
.IR i .
A client stuck on a full pool of ordinary requests still has a flush slot
of its own to send
.B Tflush
from, which is what keeps a full pool from stranding the client that
would empty it.
.PP
An
.B Rpc
moves through four states:
.B Free
(nobody owns it),
.B Queued
(a client owns it and it is on the work queue),
.B Running
(a worker is inside the handler), and
.B Done
(the reply is written and the client may take it).
Only the client ever moves a slot back to
.BR Free ,
including one that was flushed -- the tag is not free until
.B Rflush
arrives, because the only thread that knows when something may reuse it
is the one that asked for the flush.
.I Server_flushed
is the check a handler makes before it decides to abandon its own work:
true when a
.B Tflush
has named this tag, and always false on the synchronous transport, where
nothing is ever pending to flush.
.SS Workers
.I Serve_start
puts
.I n
threads on a connection, each running the same loop: wait for work, take
the head of the queue, hand it to the connection's handler, publish the
reply.
.B n
must exceed the number of requests that can block inside the handler at
once, or every worker can end up parked in one and there is nobody left
to answer the
.B Tflush
that would unstick it.
That arithmetic is a property of the server, not
of this package, and is not checked here.
One worker is the exception, and the shape a connection with no payload
arena is limited to: one request in flight behaves exactly like the
synchronous transport from the outside.
.I Serve_stop
asks the workers to leave and waits until they have, because the caller's
next act is usually to free the memory they stand on.
.SS Flush, and where its ordering rule lives
.B Tflush
has exactly one requirement: its reply is sent only after the flushed
request's fate is decided, never before and never instead.
The obvious implementation -- the worker serving the flush waits for the
original to finish -- is the wrong one, because that worker is parked
too, and enough flushes can empty every worker in the pool.
So the wait is turned inside out.
A
.B Tflush
against a request still running marks that request's
.B partner
and returns at once.
Whoever finishes the original request answers the
flush as it leaves.
No worker ever waits for another request, and no code path writes
.B Rflush
except the one that just finished the request it names.
.B Tflush
is never answered with an error: a tag that names nothing in flight, one
that already finished, and one still running all end in
.B Rflush
by the same rule, because from the client's side all three mean
.RB " " "the tag is yours again" .
.PP
The
.B abort
hook, set at
.IR init ,
is what a server does when one of its own requests is flushed.
It is
optional, and a server without one is still correct, only unable to
abandon anything, so
.B Rflush
waits for that request to finish on its own.
It is called with no lock of this package held, because waking a thread
is the server's business.
.SS The payload buffer per slot
Each request slot in the lower half owns a buffer, handed to the handler
as
.B buf
to build its reply in.
That is what makes several workers safe: a reply that borrowed shared
server storage was only ever good until that server's next message, and
with several workers the next message is already in progress.
A handler that answers out of storage of its own -- a string in
.B .rodata
-- is untouched.
Only a reply that points inside its own slot is copied
out, into the caller's own buffer, before the slot goes back.
The buffer size is the connection's
.BR msize ,
so a caller that sizes its buffer by
.B Session.msize
always has room, and a reply that still does not fit is a server bug,
reported as
.BR Short_Buffer ,
never truncated.
.SS call_for and a deadline
.I Call
waits for a reply however long that takes.
.I Call_for
is the same request with a bound in ticks.
On expiry the caller does not
simply walk away.
It sends
.B Tflush
naming its own tag from its reserved flush slot, so a full pool cannot
block the flush, and waits for
.B Rflush
uninterruptibly, because a flush of a flush has nowhere to end.
Only once
.B Rflush
arrives is the tag the caller's again, and only then is the slot's
buffer safe for the next request to reuse -- a stubborn handler may still
be writing into it long after its own client gave up.
.SS Poisoning
A connection that finds an ordinary handler bug fails one request.
A wire that finds a broken frame fails every one, because the frame
boundary is gone and there is no way to know where the next message
starts.
.I Wire_broken
reports the state.
Once set, every call on the wire fails at once rather
than parking, every slot waiting for a reply is settled as a transport
failure, and its sleeper is woken.
.B Wire_stats.hangup
distinguishes an orderly end of the byte stream from a frame that broke
protocol.
.SS The wire over a pipe
.B Wire
is the client half of a 9P connection to a program: encode a request,
write the frame, and match the reply that eventually comes back by its
tag, exactly as
.B Conn
does except that the far side is bytes rather than a procedure this
kernel can call.
.I Wire_join
waits for the one reader thread a wire has to leave, which happens when
the byte stream ends or a frame poisons the wire.
The caller's part is
to make one of those happen, ordinarily by closing the far end.
.I Wire_join_for
is the same wait with a bound, for a caller that cannot force the stream
to end.
.PP
.I Wire_flush
is
.B Tflush
sent from the reserved flush partner slot for a request abandoned by
.IR wire_call_for .
It waits up to
.B FLUSH_TICKS
(200) for
.B Rflush
and poisons the wire if nothing answers in time, because a server that
answers neither the request nor its flush has left the protocol.
The
.B Tversion
handshake that opens a session over a wire
.RB ( sys/vectra9.negotiate )
is an ordinary call with a deadline, over
.BR NOTAG ,
the one tag the protocol obliges a client to use before it has a session
at all.
A pipe's posted end becomes a wire's server the first time something
mounts the name it was posted under.
.SS The borrow rule
A handler is given the storage its reply borrows, and nothing else.
It must be safe against several threads inside it at once once it is
moved onto workers, and it must build any payload it answers with in the
buffer it is handed rather than in storage of its own that a second
caller might reach first.
Everything above this rule -- the tag pool, the flush partner, the
per-slot buffer -- exists to make that promise cheap to keep.
.SH SOURCE
.B kernel/mnt
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR vfs (9),
.IR dev (9),
.IR sync (9),
.IR pipe (2),
.IR pipe (3),
.IR vectra9 (2)
.SH BUGS
A reply string that borrows a server's own storage rather than the slot
.RB ( Rreadlink.target ", " Rgetlock.client_id )
is not copied out by
.IR deliver .
No server in the tree returns either today, so the gap is latent rather
than reachable.
